<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pennines</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pennine Hills</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pennines are a range of hills and moors running north and south through northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, from the Peak District to the Tyne Gap near the Scottish border, and often called the backbone of England. They divide the north-west from the north-east and have long shaped settlement and communications, separating kingdoms and counties on either side.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pennines.jpg" alt="Pennines" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kreuzschnabel, basemap from maps-for-free, CC0 licensed · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Topo_Map_Pennines.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
